#{[17592186519776 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519804 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519776 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519776 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/26/26fffdfc736d59a285ec7beb916417657c50a48494eace96e0db5d70cd7ce522.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519776 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ba10e69-e889-58f2-81da-5e14a75e9ef5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519776 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0a/0a8351f5cdfa59df7ec8a328c18b6e83342796728e11ff26911ce661eda1513b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519792 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519789 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=3$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=1$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "442c0b16-fa87-5944-8279-ae2d47c10e31" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519789 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1-\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519796 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519781 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519796 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519796 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519796 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54990ea5-aa3e-570c-b3da-1d09e2497f35" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519796 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 9 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519799 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519807 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519799 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519799 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59599019-d2a1-5995-abe7-1dea2959b06c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519799 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 12 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519809 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 1759